          {
            "id": "p42-q3-diagram2",
            "role": "question",
            "format": "tikz",
            "code": "% mathsmap-diagram-colours {\"version\":1,\"kind\":\"geometry\",\"base\":[],\"semantic\":[{\"name\":\"altitudeRed\",\"hex\":\"EF6068\",\"reason\":\"distinguishes the constructed perpendicular AE\"}],\"reference\":\"page 42 question 3 second diagram\"}\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm]\n\\special{dvisvgm:raw <metadata data-graph-strokes=\"1\"/>}\n\\definecolor{altitudeRed}{HTML}{EF6068}\n\\coordinate (B) at (0,0);\n\\coordinate (E) at (1,0);\n\\coordinate (D) at (1.5,0);\n\\coordinate (C) at (3,0);\n\\coordinate (A) at (1,3);\n\\draw[line width=0.8pt] (A)--(B)--(C)--cycle (A)--(D);\n\\draw[altitudeRed,dashed,line width=0.4pt] (A)--(E);\n\\draw[line width=0.4pt] (1,0.17)--(1.17,0.17)--(1.17,0);\n\\node[above] at (A) {$A$};\n\\node[below left] at (B) {$B$};\n\\node[below left] at (E) {$E$};\n\\node[below right] at (D) {$D$};\n\\node[below right] at (C) {$C$};\n\\path (A)--node[left] {$a$} (B);\n\\path (A)--node[right] {$b$} (C);\n\\path (A)--node[right] {$d$} (D);\n\\path (A)--node[left] {$h$} (E);\n\\path (E)--node[below] {$p$} (D);\n\\path (D)--node[below] {$m$} (C);\n\\end{tikzpicture}",
            "src": null,
            "widthMm": 47,
            "alt": "Mathematical diagram",
            "overlayOf": null,
            "transparent": false,
            "axes": null,
            "spec": {
              "sourcePage": 42,
              "sourceRegion": "Question 3, second triangle",
              "description": "Base points occur in order B, E, D, C. The dashed red altitude AE is perpendicular to BC; AD is the median.",
              "mathematics": "AB=a, AC=b, AD=d, AE=h, ED=p, DC=m, BD=m.",
              "answerVisibility": "Construction and all supplied labels remain visible; completed equations are responses.",
              "sizing": {
                "purpose": "supplied"
              }
            },
            "derived": false,
            "reviewStatus": "needs-review"
          }
        ],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$a^2=h^2+(m-p)^2$, $b^2=h^2+(m+p)^2$, $d^2=h^2+p^2$.",
          "worked": "Using the right-angled triangles $ABE$, $ACE$ and $ADE$, respectively:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}a^2&=h^2+(m-p)^2\\\\b^2&=h^2+(m+p)^2\\\\d^2&=h^2+p^2\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 0,
        "responseSpace": "scaffold"
      },
      {
        "id": "p42-q3-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Add the first two equations in part b above and simplify.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$a^2+b^2=2h^2+2m^2+2p^2$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}a^2+b^2&=h^2+(m-p)^2+h^2+(m+p)^2\\\\a^2+b^2&=2h^2+m^2-2mp+p^2+m^2+2mp+p^2\\\\a^2+b^2&=2h^2+2m^2+2p^2\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p42-q3-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Use the third equation in part b to deduce Apollonius’ theorem.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$a^2+b^2=2(d^2+m^2)$.\n\nSubstitute $h^2+p^2=d^2$ into the result from part c.",
          "worked": "From part b, $h^2+p^2=d^2$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}a^2+b^2&=2(h^2+p^2)+2m^2\\\\a^2+b^2&=2d^2+2m^2\\\\a^2+b^2&=2(d^2+m^2)\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p42-q3-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "What happens when the angle at $A$ is a right angle?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$d=m$: the median to the hypotenuse is half the hypotenuse.",
          "worked": "If $\\angle A=90^\\circ$, Pythagoras’ theorem gives $a^2+b^2=(2m)^2=4m^2$.\n\nBy Apollonius’ theorem,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}2(d^2+m^2)&=4m^2\\\\d^2+m^2&=2m^2\\\\d^2&=m^2\\\\d&=m\\end{align*}$$\n\nLengths are positive, so the median is half the hypotenuse.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      }
    ]
